  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/49/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_50.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The splash page tells us we're about to meet the \"most feared oriental of all time!\"&nbsp; The Mandarin has been lurking in the \"vastness of Red China,\" and Iron Man is about to face him!\n\nThe Mandarin is feared in the orient, and four high-ranking military officers of the Red government seek an audience with him.&nbsp; We get to see his castle as he agrees to hear what they have to say.&nbsp; The officials fear him, but their government has ordered it.&nbsp; They have come to ask him to share the secret of his fearsome power rings!&nbsp; But Mandarin will have none of that - he will share his secrets with <i>no</i> one!&nbsp; He grants them 60 seconds to flee for their lives!\n\nBack in the States, the military has contacted Iron Man to learn more about this \"Mandarin\" character that the red government is so fearful of, for their own security.&nbsp; Iron Man prepares to head for Red China, but he first has to excuse himself from an employee's dinner as Tony Stark, which gives us a chance to check in on Pepper and Happy (essentially allowing them to \"punch in\" for this issue - the Pepper/Happy moments are...<i>uh</i>...\"peppered\" throughout the issue).\n\nNext page we see Iron Man hopping a jet flying near Red China, and he bails out, making sure that any who see him think that no chute opened and that he plummeted to his death.&nbsp; (Heh, I love the way the Chinese are drawn here - all with slanty-eyes that never seem to open.&nbsp; <i>Wow</i>!)&nbsp; Iron Man, now on the ground, walks for hours before finally coming to \"the castle of the <i>Mandarin</i>!\"\n\nHe encounters the private guards of the Mandarin and makes short work of them, but Mandarin sees him approaching.&nbsp; A simple death trap is easily avoided, and the Mandarin welcomes Iron Man into his presence, telling him he'll never leave alive.&nbsp; (Convenient that he speaks fluent English of course - that's almost a <i>given</i> in this particular magazine, where just about <i>everyone</i> speaks perfect English!)\n\nAs Iron Man uses his gadgets to try and topple the Mandarin, the latter uses one ring after another to ward off the attacks, and Iron Man realizes each one is a different weapon, and the \"paralyzer ray\" soon has Iron Man frozen in his tracks!&nbsp; But Iron Man recovers faster than expected, and the Mandarin then reveals that he's also the \"greatest karate master the world has ever known.\"&nbsp; As he approaches to finish off Iron Man with his bare hands, the armor on his left arm opens to reveal Iron Man's \"built-in slide rule calculator\" (<i>Yeah baby</i>!&nbsp; Every armor should be equipped with this gadget!&nbsp; <i>Hahahahahaha</i><i>!</i>)&nbsp; Even Mandarin is \"so amused by Iron Man's apparent mood of madness\" that he has to pause.&nbsp; (And who can blame him for being astonished at a \"slide rule calculator\" in this advanced suit of armor?)\n\nBut Iron Man was using the calculator to determine the exact angle to ward off the eventual blow so that Mandarin's arm would be hurt.&nbsp; With him crumpled in pain, Iron Man heads out to catch his \"pick-up plane.\"\n\nHe's able to get back, just in time to attend part of the employee's dinner after all, and Pepper is annoyed now because she decided to attend it with Happy and now thinks the boss won't look her way if he thinks the two are a couple!\n\nBut the Mandarin sits stewing in his castle, and we're left \"wondering when and where the powerful oriental menace will <i>strike</i> again!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "I'll admit right up front that, while I am a <i>huge</i> fan of Iron Man, I have <i>never</i> been a fan of the Mandarin.&nbsp; The ten rings have always seemed to me to be a crutch for the author and are too tempting to use as \"what power can we pull out of the hat next\" trick gimmick.&nbsp; (Not to mention that I've never been big on stories that feature far-eastern threats, as the whole judo/karate thing is just not for me in general.)&nbsp; This is the first time I've ever bothered to sit down and read the issue where he makes his first appearance.\n\nMandarin is clearly a formidable foe, feared even by his own government, but it also seems like Red China realizes he's a threat to <i>them</i> also, and you have to wonder why they would not attend to this nearby menace themselves, rather than leaving him free to plot who-knows-what.&nbsp; The Red rulers would have the ability to just order troops in there to overwhelm him, but I know we don't want to overthink it here....\n\nPepper and Happy, as we so often find, appear shoe-horned into the story simply because we <i>expect</i> them to appear, even though the story really required that we focus on Iron Man, and two more pages of him taking on the Mandarin, I think would have been better than cramming them into this tale needlessly after their initial appearance near the beginning.\n\nAnd what about Iron Man's report back to the US government officials?&nbsp; They don't even mention that!&nbsp; One can only assume he explained that the US should indeed be worried about the Mandarin...but <i>now</i> what?\n\nI noted on the top-right panel of page 6, the close-up of Iron Man's fist, which ultimately becomes one of the hallmark looks for the character once George Tuska picks up the art duties, in what I call the \"disembodied fist punch!\"&nbsp; This was the first time I've seen the look, done here by Don Heck, but <i>boy</i> did it bring back a lot of fond memories of the later Tuska issues!\n\nThe \"built-in slide rule calculator\" was so funny that I awarded a half-point for that alone.&nbsp; I know this was published in the 1960s, and calculators debuted later, but that was so unexpected and funny that it gets a retroactive \"VMA\" (Vintage Moment Award)!&nbsp; And I hereby coin this term for the sake of these reviews.&nbsp; (<i>Writing later on 1/6/2024 - this was the \"origin\" of the VMA</i>.)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe grade would have hovered in the 5.0 to 5.5 realm, but as I said, the VMA gets it up to full mid-grade.\n\n(I'll promise to try and keep an open mind with regard to the Mandarin and his all-too-many upcoming appearances in these pages.)",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher</b> (\"Journey's End\"):\nWe hear the story of Wilbur Weems, ridiculed by his fellow man in the 21<sup>st</sup> century, who signs up for an obvious one-way mission into space to investigate a strange radiation that could interfere with space travel.&nbsp; The volunteer pilot has no chance of returning.&nbsp; Since he has the least to lose, Weems volunteers.&nbsp; He actually gets to the \"next galaxy\" where he is transformed into a hunk by the people who set up the radiation to test for brave adventurers who would go even with no chance of returning.&nbsp; He's reached paradise, and the Watcher turns toward the reader to encourage us to boldly go about our daily tasks, knowing that we are never alone!\n\nOkay, this is clearly becoming a bully-pulpit for the writers here - a \"tale of suspense\" that has a moral at the end.&nbsp; Not sure if this is the best use of the Watcher, but at least it's somewhat in character...."
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}